  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nFast Piecewise Optimization for Erdos Minimum Overlap Problem\nThe current algorithm relies on exhaustive local searches within a predefined grid, leading to slow convergence and local maxima traps. This strategy replaces it with a piecewise optimization approach that:\n- Uses adaptive grid refinement to fix function values on intervals rather than points\n- Optimizes the overlap integral using Lagrange multiplier method for the integral constraint\n- Employs caching and analytical properties of the correlation function to reduce computation\n\nThe key bottleneck is the non-convex optimization over a high-dimensional space. This approach reduces dimensionality by using piecewise constant representations, speeds up computation with quadratic interpolation, and escapes local minima through extremal shift operators.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation:\nSplit [0,2] into K intervals, maintaining exact solution continuity. Represent h as:\nh_i(x) = 1.0 if x in [a_i, b_i) else 0.0\nWith normalized measure: sum_i (b_i - a_i) = 2\n\nOptimization variables:\n- Breakpoints positions x\u2081, x\u2082, ..., x_{K-1}\n- Piece values y = [y\u2081, y\u2082, ..., y_K] (0 or 1)\n- Integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = sum_i \u222b_I h_i(x)dx = sum_i (y_i * l_i) = 1\n\nObjective:\nmin_h max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nDerived forms:\nUse valid-extremal-shift property to analyze lag maxima:\nWhen a lag k shifts h(x)(1-h(x+k)) sharply to 0, the product correlation vanishes.\n\nConstraints:\n0 \u2264 \u222bh(x)dx \u2264 2, but normalized to 1.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nParent validation:\n- If n_points not divisible by 2, maintain parent feasibility via projection\n- If initial_h_values exists, project to ensure integral\u22481\n\nCore steps:\nFamily construction:\nFix K intervals (default K=10)\n(current_step in range(30):\nIf timeout: revert for fallback\n    \n    Initialize either:\n        - Parent transfer from initial_h_values\n        - Random feasible points\n    \n    Refinement update:\n        Calculate interval midpoints, value shifts\n        Cache Extremal detection points (high correlation intensities)\n        \n    Optimization loop:\n        Use quadratic interpolation for best moves\n        Apply extremal shift operators at cache hits\n        Maintain interval normalizations:\n            Sum(l_i) must equal n_points/2 (prev adjusted)\n        \n    Permutation selection:\n        If improvement breakout based on extremal shift:\n            Maintain winner with slightly mutated grid\n        Otherwise:\n            Maintain centroid mean squared error\n            Apply structured Perturbations:\n                - Shift break intervals\n                - Flip interval values\n\n4. Search choices\nIntrinsic adaptations:\n- Minimal evaluations (\u2264dx\u00b2) per catch\n- Error-controlled extremal detection (log-barrier \u03b1=0.9, \u03b2=0.1)\n- Parallel processing if available (>2 workers)\n\nFallback procedure:\n- Maintain parent recovery plan\n- Integral normalize via rebinning\n- Extremal-shift penalty threshold 0.99",
